id: pi
name: Pi

Register llm.birks.dev as a custom OpenAI-compatible provider in Pi's model configuration (earendil-works/pi, ~/.pi/agent/models.json).

--- file ~/.pi/agent/models.json (json) ---
{
  "providers": {
    "llm-birks-dev": {
      "baseUrl": "https://llm.birks.dev/v1",
      "api": "openai-completions",
      "apiKey": "$LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY",
      "models": [
        {
          "id": "Qwen3-Coder-30B",
          "name": "Qwen3-Coder-30B",
          "reasoning": false,
          "contextWindow": 262144,
          "maxTokens": 8192
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}

--- commands (sh) ---
export LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"

pi

--- commands (PowerShell) ---
$env:LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY = "YOUR_API_KEY"

pi

--- notes ---
- The $LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY form tells Pi to expand the credential from your environment, so the key never lands in the file.
- The openai-completions API sends Authorization: Bearer, which is what the gateway expects.
- If you already have a models.json, merge the providers entry rather than replacing the file.

--- agent prompt ---
Configure Pi on this machine to use my self-hosted llm.birks.dev endpoint at https://llm.birks.dev. Add a custom provider to ~/.pi/agent/models.json using the openai-completions API, expanding the API key from an environment variable, merging with any existing providers. Use the model "Qwen3-Coder-30B". Read the API key from the LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY environment variable. Do not write the key itself into any file in a git repository, and do not print it. Preserve my existing unrelated configuration.
